Many of us might wonderwhat is the function of cactus spines if apparently they are stems that seem to have no use for plants, right?
2 What functions do they have?
Cacti are plants that have evolved over 40 million years, since the Paleozoic era. At the beginning they did have leaves, as do those of the Pereskia genus (the most primitive of all), but little by little, as the climate became drier as America separated from Africa and moved towards its present location, they were losing them.
Good because to feed the leaves it is necessary to have water available, and when the precious liquid is scarce, there is no other option but to adapt or disappear. It is the law of natural selection, and against that plant beings by themselves cannot do anything.
Cactus spines serve several functions, which are as follows:
Provide shade: it is true that not much, but it is enough so that the loss of water by evaporation is minimal.
Direct the water towards the fleshy part of the cactus: dew drops settle on all aerial parts of plants. By doing so, the pores open up and can be hydrated.
