The normal thickness of the coating of most circuit board products in PCBA processing is 25 to 127 microns, and the coating thickness of some products is lower. The specific thickness should be determined according to the actual use scenario and customer requirements.
Circuit boards must use the thinnest possible coating material to provide maximum protection to minimize heat retention, additional weight gain, and various other issues. There are three main ways to measure the thickness of a conformal coating.
These gauges contain a series of notches, each tooth having a known calibrated length. Place the gauge directly on the wet film to take a thin film measurement, then multiply this measurement by the percent solids of the coating to calculate the approximate dry coating thickness.
The standard deviation of the measurements is then calculated to determine the uniformity of the coating. Micrometer measurements are best done with a harder coating that won’t deform under pressure.
For example, for ordinary electronic products, the requirements for conformal paint are not particularly high, and there is no special thickness requirement when coating, generally a thickness of 20-30 microns can be satisfied. If there are special requirements, multiple coatings can be taken, and the second coating can be carried out after the conformal paint on the board is completely cured.
