In most products that do not require miniaturization and high power, through-hole or mixed technology circuit boards are still used, such as TVs, home audio and video equipment, etc., still use through-hole components, so wave soldering is required. From a process point of view, wave soldering machines can only provide some basic equipment operating parameter adjustments.
After the PCBA has been wave soldered, cleaned, stored and repaired, there may be whitening around the pads. These white substances are mainly caused by residues.
2) Inappropriate preheating temperature or curve parameters;
Reasons for the whitening of PCBA pads after cleaning:
Most of the white substances produced after cleaning, storage, and failure of solder joints are rosin inherent in the flux itself.
This is a substance produced by the reaction of rosin and flux during the soldering process of the board, and the solubility of this substance is generally poor, so it is not easy to be cleaned, and stays on the board to form a white residue.
The principle of removing oxides on the welding surface is that organic acids react with metal oxides to form metal salts soluble in liquid rosin, which form a solid solution with rosin after cooling, and are removed together with rosin during cleaning.
High frequency and high load operation will have continuous high impact on the circuit and components of the circuit board, and it is more likely to be oxidized under the influence of heating, resulting in short circuit and open circuit during long-term operation. Therefore, the working parameters of the PCBA board should be in the middle range of the components to avoid close to the peak value, so as to effectively protect the PCBA and prolong its storage life.
